</mj-text>
    <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2
    style="
      font-size: 24px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin-top: 2px !important;
      margin: 0;
    "
  >
    Market strike horror
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    <mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  target="_blank"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>
    
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What happened?</strong> A Nigerian military airstrike hit Tumfa market in Zamfara State, <a href="https://www.dw.com/en/nigeria-airstrike-kills-100-at-market-amnesty-says/a-77135993" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">killing at least 100 civilians</a>, according to Amnesty International. The rights group said many victims were women and girls, and called for an immediate investigation into what it described as reckless attacks on civilians. The Red Cross also confirmed civilian deaths, while local leaders said dozens of injured people were being treated in nearby hospitals. Nigeria’s military denied the reports.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Repeated deaths:</strong> Repeated civilian deaths in Nigerian air operations have drawn growing criticism from rights groups and local communities. Amnesty International said the Tumfa market strike follows another deadly market airstrike last month in Jilli, northeastern Nigeria, where around 200 civilians were reportedly killed. The group said civilians in conflict-hit northern regions have too often carried the heaviest burden of military operations, even as security forces target bandits and jihadist-linked groups.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Big picture: </strong>Nigeria’s fight against bandit gangs and jihadist-linked groups is increasingly putting civilians at risk. In northern regions, armed groups often operate near villages, roads, and markets, making it difficult for civilians to avert danger. Rights groups say repeated civilian deaths show the need for stronger intelligence, clearer accountability, and better protection for ordinary people.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
