<mj-section background-color="#eff1f4" padding="1px 0.5px 1px 1px" border-radius="5px">
  <mj-column background-color="#fff" border-radius="5px" padding="0px">
    
    <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2
    style="
      font-size: 24px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin-top: 2px !important;
      margin: 0;
    "
  >
    De-risking gets risky
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    <mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  target="_blank"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>
    
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What happened?</strong> China is making it harder for Western firms to reduce dependence on Chinese supply chains. Beijing recently blocked Meta’s $2B takeover of AI startup Manus, even though the company is based in Singapore, because it has strong Chinese roots. China also introduced the latest supply chain security rules that could punish foreign firms for moving production to countries such as India or Vietnam, for reshoring factories, or for following US and EU sanctions against Chinese entities.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Supply chain rules: </strong>China’s <a href="https://www.dw.com/en/chinas-new-rules-give-the-west-a-new-headache/a-77075741" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">Regulations on Industrial and Supply Chain Security</a> give Beijing wider power to push back against Western de-risking. Firms could also face fines or supply chain blacklisting if they comply with US or EU sanctions and export controls targeting Chinese entities. Analysts say the rules are designed to make decoupling harder and to keep foreign companies dependent on China’s technology, manufacturing, and critical-mineral supply chains.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What is de-risking?</strong> De-risking means reducing dependence on China without fully cutting ties. Western governments and companies use it to make supply chains safer by relocating some production, sourcing parts from other countries, or reshoring key industries. Since the pandemic, the US and EU have pushed harder to reduce reliance on China for critical goods, technology, and minerals. But Beijing sees this as a threat to its economic influence.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="10px" />
    <!-- </mj-section> -->
    <!-- <mj-spacer height="10px" /> -->
